## std::future — Future / Poll / Context / Waker surface.
##
## Sub-phase 1 of the async round (docs/specs/stdlib/async.spec.md):
## pure stdlib surface, no executor, no `async def` lowering. The
## types declared here let user code write `include Future` /
## `type Output = T` / `def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[T]`
## and have it type-check. Running anything that calls into the
## Waker / Context C runtime panics with "executor not yet
## implemented" — sub-phase 3 fills in the real implementations.
##
## Why a separate package: the Future mixin signature references
## Poll AND Context, and Poll is generic over the future's Output.
## Co-locating the three keeps the cross-references inside one
## bootstrap-loaded file and avoids ordering surprises between
## `core.rvn` (Future mixin's old home) and the executor runtime.

## Poll[T] — the result of polling a Future. Tag layout is fixed:
##   Ready = 0, Pending = 1
## Pinned by `poll_tag_layout_stability` in
## `compiler/riven_core/tests/async_surface.rs`. DO NOT reorder;
## append new variants at the END only (no current need, kept here
## for parity with the IoError contract).
enum Poll[T]
  Ready(T)
  Pending
end

## Future — the mixin every async type implements. Sub-phase 1
## defines the surface only; sub-phase 2 lowers `async def` / `.await`
## to state machines that satisfy this shape. Implementors:
##
##   class CountdownFuture
##     remaining: Int
##     include Future
##     type Output = Int
##
##     def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Int]
##       if self.remaining == 0
##         Poll.Ready(0)
##       else
##         self.remaining = self.remaining - 1
##         Poll.Pending
##       end
##     end
##   end
## `dispatch runtime` — see docs/specs/types/mixin_vtables.spec.md.
## Future is the v1 reference case for vtable-based runtime dispatch:
## the executor / scheduler queues heterogeneous futures and polls
## each via `Future_dynamic_poll(self, cx)` rather than a static
## `<ConcreteClass>_poll`. Every class that includes Future gets an
## 8-byte `class_info_ptr` header at heap offset 0, populated at
## construction time with the class's `__rvn_classinfo_<Class>`.
mixin Future dispatch runtime
  type Output
  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Self.Output]
end

## Context — the executor-supplied polling context. Holds an opaque
## pointer the executor maintains; user code only ever calls
## `cx.waker` to obtain the wake handle.
##
## Sub-phase 3 of the async round (executor.spec.md B4) wired
## `cx.waker` to return a real singleton no-op Waker (no longer
## panics). The mechanical "wake" channel is inert until sub-phase
## 4 introduces genuine park points (async I/O on epoll/kqueue).
##
## Context.test_dummy: a hand-rolled no-op context used by the
## async-lowering pin tests (async_lowering.spec.md B6 — Milestone
## 2A). The dummy and the executor-made context are wire-compatible
## — both carry the singleton no-op waker, so `cx.waker()` works on
## either kind.
##
## Context.executor: factory used by the compiler intrinsic
## `block_on` rewriter (executor.spec.md B1, B4). Returns a heap-
## allocated context whose waker is the same singleton no-op as
## test_dummy. v1 sub-phase 3 has no per-task wake state, so a
## single shared instance per call is fine.
## Context — the executor-supplied polling context. Sub-phase 4A
## (docs/specs/stdlib/async_io.spec.md B3) wires the per-thread
## reactor through `Context.executor` (which allocates and installs
## the reactor on the current thread) and `Context.drop` (which
## tears it down at Context scope exit). The reactor itself is
## thread-local in the C runtime — see
## library/std/future/runtime/reactor.c — so the AST-level block_on
## rewriter's existing `Thread.yield_now` call in the Pending arm
## now drives the reactor's wait point without any compiler change.
class Context
  lib "runtime/executor.c"
    def waker as "riven_context_waker"(self) -> &Waker
    def self.test_dummy as "riven_context_test_dummy"() -> Context
    def self.executor as "riven_executor_make_context"() -> Context
    def drop as "riven_executor_context_drop"(self) -> ()
  end
end

## Waker — handle a Future stashes to notify the executor when it
## becomes pollable again. Two methods: `wake` (consumes the waker)
## and `wake_by_ref` (borrowed).
##
## Sub-phase 3 of the async round (executor.spec.md B3): both
## methods are no-ops. The poll loop in block_on spins
## continuously (with Thread.yield_now between iterations), so any
## "wake" is implicit. Sub-phase 4 wires real signaling when async
## I/O introduces genuine blocking park points.
class Waker
  lib "runtime/executor.c"
    def wake as "riven_waker_wake"(self) -> ()
    def wake_by_ref as "riven_waker_wake_by_ref"(self) -> ()
  end
end

## --- Sub-phase 4A: reactor free-fn lib decls -----------------------
##
## Spec: docs/specs/stdlib/async_io.spec.md Milestone 4A.
##
## The four reactor primitives in C (runtime/reactor.c) are declared
## here as free-fn lib decls so the user-side `TimeSleepFuture`
## (below) reaches them through the FFI alias map.
##
## 4A landed in two steps. Commit `42daaeb` shipped the C reactor +
## per-thread lifecycle (Context.executor / Context.drop) + the
## `Thread.yield_now` → `riven_reactor_park_current` indirection.
## The user-side Async.sleep / TimeSleepFuture follow here.
lib "runtime/reactor.c"
  def riven_reactor_current_handle() -> Int
  def riven_reactor_register_timer(reactor: Int, nanos: Int) -> Int
  def riven_reactor_check_fired(reactor: Int, handle: Int) -> Int
  def riven_reactor_deregister(reactor: Int, handle: Int) -> ()
end

## Monotonic nanos free fn used by `TimeSleepFuture` to compute its
## absolute deadline. Cross-package lib decl pointing at the C symbol
## owned by std.time's runtime/time.c.
lib "../../time/runtime/time.c"
  def riven_time_now_ns() -> Int
end

## --- TimeSleepFuture (Milestone 4A B1) -----------------------------
##
## Pure-Riven hand-written future. Resolves after `d` has elapsed.
## Drives the reactor's wait point indirectly: returning Pending
## from `poll` parks the block_on loop on `Thread.yield_now`, whose
## C body (`riven_thread_yield` in library/std/time/runtime/time.c)
## delegates to `riven_reactor_park_current` when the reactor has
## registered timers. When the timer fires, the parked thread wakes,
## the executor re-polls, and `riven_reactor_check_fired` flips the
## handle's "fired" bit and lets the future return Ready.
##
## Why two fields (deadline + handle): `init` runs eagerly at
## `Async.sleep(d)` call time, before `poll` first runs. The reactor
## is per-block_on and may not exist yet at construction time. We
## compute the absolute deadline at init — a wall-clock-independent
## monotonic point — and defer reactor registration until first poll,
## when a reactor is guaranteed to exist (Context.executor has
## already installed it on the current thread). `handle == 0` is the
## sentinel for "not yet registered".
class TimeSleepFuture
  deadline_nanos: Int
  handle: Int
  include Future
  type Output = ()

  def init(d: &Duration)
    self.deadline_nanos = riven_time_now_ns() + d.as_nanos()
    self.handle = 0
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[()]
    # First poll: compute remaining and register with the reactor.
    if self.handle == 0
      let remaining = self.deadline_nanos - riven_time_now_ns()
      if remaining <= 0
        return Poll.Ready(())
      end
      self.handle = riven_reactor_register_timer(riven_reactor_current_handle(), remaining)
      return Poll.Pending
    end
    # Subsequent polls: ask the reactor whether the timer fired.
    if riven_reactor_check_fired(riven_reactor_current_handle(), self.handle) != 0
      Poll.Ready(())
    else
      Poll.Pending
    end
  end

  def drop
    # Idempotent cleanup. `deregister` is safe with handle == 0 (no
    # slot match → no-op), but we gate it anyway to avoid the
    # find-slot walk on the common drop-before-register path.
    if self.handle != 0
      riven_reactor_deregister(riven_reactor_current_handle(), self.handle)
    end
  end
end

## --- Async namespace (sub-phase 4A B1) -----------------------------
##
## The user-facing async runtime surface. `Async.sleep(d)` returns a
## TimeSleepFuture that resolves after `d` has elapsed; usable from
## both sync code via `block_on(Async.sleep(d))` and async code via
## `Async.sleep(d).await` (per async_io.spec.md B-Z).
##
## Why a class rather than a free fn: class-static-method dispatch
## is unambiguous about the async runtime surface (cf. discussion
## around naming — sync stays on `Thread.sleep`, async lives on
## `Async.*`). Future additions land here as more class-statics.
class Async
  def self.sleep(d: &Duration) -> TimeSleepFuture
    TimeSleepFuture.new(d)
  end
end

## --- Sub-phase 5: task scheduler (commit 1) ------------------------
##
## Spec: docs/specs/stdlib/task_spawn.spec.md.
##
## Commit 1 ships the queue + spawn + yield_now + raw handle
## accessors. Commit 2 layers `task_join(h).await` over this surface
## via the existing free-fn `.await` desugar. The TaskHandle is held
## as an opaque `Int` at the Riven surface, mirroring the convention
## established by `JoinHandle.join_raw(handle: Int)` in library/std/
## sync/src/lib.rvn (an OS-thread join handle).
##
## Trade-off documented in the brief: a typed `class TaskHandle[T]`
## value-class would need lib-decl support for `&var Future` /
## `self` ↔ `Int` cast at the call boundary. v1 ships at the raw
## Int level (same as Thread.spawn_raw). Commit 2 introduces a typed
## __TaskJoinFuture[T] that recovers T at the join site.

## Class Task — surface for Task.spawn / Task.yield_now.
##
## `Task.spawn_raw(future_ptr: Int) -> Int` enqueues a Riven future
## (the i64 heap pointer of a class that `include Future dispatch
## runtime`) and returns the C-side TaskHandle pointer as i64. The
## helper does NOT poll the future — that happens later, during the
## pump call the block_on inline loop emits between iterations.
##
## v1 type laxness: callers pass a future-typed value where `Int` is
## declared. Riven's existing FFI codegen treats both at i64 (same
## pattern as `Thread.spawn_raw(closure: Int)` — the closure value
## flows in as a pointer). Type-safety improvement (typed
## `Task.spawn(fut: &var Future) -> TaskHandle[T]`) lands in commit 2
## once lib-decl grammar can express dyn-mixin params.
##
## `Task.yield_now -> TaskYieldFuture` is a static constructor.
class Task
  lib "runtime/scheduler.c"
    def self.spawn_raw as "riven_executor_spawn"(future_ptr: Int) -> Int
  end

  def self.yield_now -> TaskYieldFuture
    TaskYieldFuture.new()
  end
end

## TaskHandle namespace class for static-method handle ops. Same
## pattern as `JoinHandle` in library/std/sync — handle is held as
## `Int` at the user surface; ops are static methods that take the
## raw handle. v1 surface:
##   TaskHandle.is_done(h)  -> Int (1 if ready, 0 if pending)
##   TaskHandle.result(h)   -> Int (valid only when is_done returns 1)
##   TaskHandle.drop_handle(h) -> ()  (releases caller-side refcount)
##
## `drop_handle` is named explicitly (not `drop`) because Riven's
## `def drop` discipline reserves that name for class-instance drop
## hooks. Here we're acting on a raw Int handle — closer to a free
## function in spirit. Match the JoinHandle.join_raw shape.
class TaskHandle
  lib "runtime/scheduler.c"
    def self.is_done as "riven_task_handle_is_done"(h: Int) -> Int
    def self.result as "riven_task_handle_result"(h: Int) -> Int
    def self.drop_handle as "riven_task_handle_drop"(h: Int) -> ()
  end
end

## Scheduler internal entries. These exist as module-level lib decls
## so the AST-level block_on rewriter can synthesise calls to them
## via a plain `Identifier(...)` reference (matches how Thread.yield_now
## gets emitted from the Pending arm today).
##
## `riven_executor_pump_tasks` is called by the block_on inline loop
## once per iteration when the queue is non-empty (cheap: a single
## thread-local pointer compare via `riven_executor_queue_nonempty`).
## `riven_executor_drain_remaining` would be called by Context.drop —
## but plumbing through Context.drop requires a follow-up commit; v1
## ships with the leak-on-block_on-exit behaviour acknowledged in
## scheduler.c's header. Tasks that complete cleanly via the pump
## free themselves; only abandoned tasks leak.
lib "runtime/scheduler.c"
  def riven_executor_pump_tasks() -> Int
  def riven_executor_queue_nonempty() -> Int
  def riven_executor_drain_remaining() -> ()
end

## TaskYieldFuture — cooperative yield. First poll returns Pending,
## second poll returns Ready. Holds a single "did I yield yet?" bit.
##
## Same one-field shape as TimeSleepFuture (no generic, single-state
## bookkeeping). Construction is zero-arg.
class TaskYieldFuture
  polled: Int
  include Future
  type Output = ()

  def init
    self.polled = 0
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[()]
    if self.polled == 0
      self.polled = 1
      Poll.Pending
    else
      Poll.Ready(())
    end
  end
end
